#
# Put a real box into the running world, so the lidar sees it and the
# costmaps have to plan around it.
#
# Served as /faults/spawn_obstacle by ral_faults/fault_manager.py (Phase 4.1).
# Injection point: /world/<world>/create (gz UserCommands) - the entity is
# created in the simulator, not drawn on a map.
#
# `pose` is in the gz WORLD frame, which is the frame gz entity poses are
# defined in. It is NOT converted through world_to_map; only /initialpose and
# the Nav2 goal are.
#
# `size` is the full extent (x, y, z) of the box in metres. Every extent must
# be positive and every numeric field finite; the manager refuses the request
# otherwise rather than asking gz to build an impossible box.
#
# `name` must match [A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,63}. It is substituted into the
# SDF the manager sends to gz, inside `<model name="...">`, so a name carrying
# XML would inject arbitrary content - a plugin element included - into the
# running world, and a bare `&` or `<` would corrupt the document into a
# confusing gz refusal. The manager refuses anything outside that set.
#
# `fault_id` is the join key for this fault instance: the value the caller will
# see again on every FaultEvent the manager publishes for it. The scenario
# runner sends the id it minted (<kind>_<1-based index after sorting by
# at_sec>, scenario.py:308). An EMPTY string means "allocate one", which is
# what the dashboard sends; the manager answers with <kind>_ui_<n>. The
# RESPONSE carries the id the manager used - the one sent, or the minted one
# when the request left it empty - so a caller can join its own request to the
# events it caused without racing /fault_events.
#
# `success` is true only when gz itself accepted the entity; `message` carries
# gz's own reply verbatim when it did not.

string fault_id
string name
geometry_msgs/Pose pose
geometry_msgs/Vector3 size
---
string fault_id
bool success
string message
